## Recap: the Week 1 axial bar

![Axially loaded bar with length, area, material and applied force labelled](../week01/images/axial_bar_problem.png)<!-- width="75%" -->

For the Week 1 bar, $E=200{,}000$ MPa, $A=10$ mm², $L=1000$ mm and $F=5000$ N.

**Predict first:** How many nodes, axial displacement DOFs and elements are in the simplest truss model?

{{1}}
********************
The model has **two nodes**, **two axial displacement DOFs** before supports are applied, and **one element**. Fixing the left node leaves one unknown displacement.
********************

## One-element stiffness equation

For a straight 1D truss element, define its axial stiffness as $k=EA/L$. The element equation is

$$
\begin{bmatrix}f_1\\f_2\end{bmatrix}
=\frac{EA}{L}
\begin{bmatrix}1&-1\\-1&1\end{bmatrix}
\begin{bmatrix}u_1\\u_2\end{bmatrix}.
$$

The matrix is **2 × 2** because the element has two axial displacement DOFs.

{{1}}
********************
For the Week 1 numbers, $EA/L=2000$ N/mm. With $u_1=0$ and $f_2=5000$ N, the free-end displacement is $u_2=2.5$ mm, matching the direct calculation $FL/(EA)$.
********************

## Two elements connected in series

Consider two axial elements connecting nodes **1–2** and **2–3**. They share node 2, so the whole model has three axial displacement DOFs: $u_1$, $u_2$ and $u_3$.

Let $k_1=E_1A_1/L_1$ and $k_2=E_2A_2/L_2$. Sketch the free-body diagram and place each element's stiffness contribution into the global matrix before revealing it.

{{1}}
********************
$$
\begin{bmatrix}f_1\\f_2\\f_3\end{bmatrix}
=
\begin{bmatrix}
k_1&-k_1&0\\
-k_1&k_1+k_2&-k_2\\
0&-k_2&k_2
\end{bmatrix}
\begin{bmatrix}u_1\\u_2\\u_3\end{bmatrix}.
$$

The centre diagonal term contains **both** element contributions because node 2 belongs to both elements.
********************

## Apply the support and check the result

Fix node 1, so $u_1=0$. If the only applied external load is $F$ at node 3, write the equations for the two unknown displacements before solving.

{{1}}
********************
$$
\begin{bmatrix}
k_1+k_2&-k_2\\
-k_2&k_2
\end{bmatrix}
\begin{bmatrix}u_2\\u_3\end{bmatrix}
=\begin{bmatrix}0\\F\end{bmatrix}.
$$

For two rods in series under the same axial force, the total end displacement is the sum of their extensions:

$$u_3=\frac{FL_1}{E_1A_1}+\frac{FL_2}{E_2A_2}.$$

Compare the sign and size of the ABAQUS displacement with this hand calculation. The support reaction should balance the applied force.
********************

